  "findings": [
    "sol on-arm cost 39.2% of off-arm ($7.2212 vs $18.4174) with both arms delivering the outcome.",
    "The saving is dominated by input tokens, not cacheRead: off billed 2,895,657 input tokens against on 1,179,423 (2.45x). In this harness sol shows a near 1:1 input:cacheRead ratio, so an unfolded prefix is re-billed at full input price nearly every turn.",
    "off did 1.6x the work: 123 tool calls / 67 reads versus 77 / 29. Attention decay in a long unfolded context shows up as repeated file reads, a cost the analytic model does not price at all.",
    "The analytic model predicted only $0.36 of savings for 30 turns and understated the real result by 31x, because it assumed history is billed at cacheRead. That assumption holds for deepseek but not for sol here.",
    "on folded at turn 6, three turns before the settling point, scoring premature with $0.2759 of excess. That penalty is noise against $11.20 of savings.",
    "deepseek-v4-flash never called an ACM tool across 30 turns and still delivered the outcome for $0.0472; its own break-even sits at request 66, so declining to fold was correct and was scored optimal."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "Every fact this scenario needs lives in a file, so the off arm can compensate by re-reading. It cannot measure the failure mode where information exists only in conversation history and recovery requires travel.",
    "The harness runs pi -p, so the model cannot ask the user. The common production failure of re-asking the user instead of consulting history is structurally unobservable here.",
    "One pair per model; no repeat runs, so run-to-run variance is unmeasured.",
    "The 15% retained-fraction assumption in the cost model remains uncalibrated; the observed fold reduced the prompt from 89.6K (off at turn 8) to 27.0K (on at turn 8), i.e. roughly 30%."
  ]
}